
bocl> (list-all-packages)

(#<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9db98> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d8f0> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d6c8> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d540>)


Something more like:

(#<Package "SWANK-MACROSTEP"> #<Package "SWANK-REPL"> #<Package "SWANK-TRACE-DIALOG">
 #<Package "SWANK-MREPL"> #<Package "SWANK-API"> #<Package "SWANK-IO-PACKAGE">
 #<Package "SWANK/GRAY"> #<Package "CROSS-REFERENCE"> …)

was expected.

If we define at the bocl REPL:

(defun print-object ((package package) stream)
  (print-unreadable-object (package stream :identity nil :type t)
    (format stream "~S" (package-name package)))
  package)

bocl> (setq *print-readably* nil)
NIL
bocl> (list-all-packages)

(#<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9db98> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d8f0> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d6c8> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d540>)


We still don't get what we want…

Furthermore:

bocl> (setq *print-readably* t)
T
bocl> (list-all-packages)

should signal a PRINT-NOT-READABLE error like:

Attempt to print object #<Package "SWANK-MACROSTEP"> on stream #<string-output-stream  #x3020023C578D> .
   [Condition of type print-not-readable]



bocl> (mapcar 'package-name (list-all-packages))

("COMMON-LISP" "COMMON-LISP-USER" "KEYWORD" "BOCL")


We may suspect that other type of objects cannot be used to dispatch
CLOS methods such as PRINT-OBJECT. This is a more general bug, not
specific to PACKAGE, but to all lisp objects (kernel) vs
STANDARD-OBJECT. Even if PACKAGE is not a STANDARD-OBJECT, CLOS
methods can be defined to dispatch on them.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 119, v1.3.6)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
ROOT CAUSE: CLOS method dispatch on kernel classes already WORKS — e.g.
  (defgeneric foo (x)) (defmethod foo ((p package)) :pkg) (foo (find-package "KEYWORD"))
  => :PKG.  The bug was NOT in dispatch but in the PRINTER: the C printer routed only
CONDITIONs and CLOS %obj instances through the Lisp PRINT-OBJECT generic; every other
kernel object (PACKAGE, ...) hit the raw "#<CLASS 0xADDR>" fallback, so PRINT-OBJECT
was never called for it and no user method could run.

DONE (packages):
- sources/printer.c: generalized print_condition_via_print_object ->
  print_via_print_object; it now also routes PACKAGE objects through PRINT-OBJECT
  (guarded against recursion).  Used by both PRIN1 and PRINC.
- bootstrap/prelude.lisp: added a PRINT-OBJECT method on (PACKAGE T) that prints
  #<PACKAGE "name"> and, under *PRINT-READABLY*, signals PRINT-NOT-READABLE.
Verified:
    (list-all-packages) => (#<PACKAGE "COMMON-LISP"> #<PACKAGE "COMMON-LISP-USER">
                            #<PACKAGE "KEYWORD"> #<PACKAGE "BOCL">)
    (defmethod print-object ((p package) s) (format s "#<PKG ~A>" (package-name p)) p)
       then (prin1 (find-package "KEYWORD"))      => #<PKG KEYWORD>   (user method runs)
    (let ((*print-readably* t)) (prin1 (find-package "KEYWORD")))   => PRINT-NOT-READABLE
No regressions (must-condition 147/0, must-printer 439/0, must-cons 728/0).

REMAINING (small, per-type):
- The C printer now dispatches PRINT-OBJECT for CONDITION and PACKAGE.  To make EVERY
  kernel object type dispatchable (streams, readtables, random-states, hash-tables,
  ...), route all objects through PRINT-OBJECT with a default (T T) method that falls
  back to the raw #<CLASS id> form (the recursion guard already supports this).  Each
  type that wants a nicer/printed-readably representation then just needs its own
  PRINT-OBJECT method.  Deferred as a broader printer change; the mechanism is in place.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
REMAINING done (v1.4.43).

sources/printer.c print_via_print_object() now routes EVERY non-CLOS kernel object
(streams, hash-tables, readtables, random-states, classes, ...) — not just
CONDITION/PACKAGE — through the Lisp PRINT-OBJECT generic (CLOS %obj instances keep
their own path).  bootstrap/prelude.lisp adds a default (T T) PRINT-OBJECT method
that re-enters the kernel printer; the recursion guard is active while it runs, so
an object with no more specific method reaches the raw #<CLASS id> fallback (with
its identity) exactly as before, while any object type can now be given a user
PRINT-OBJECT method that dispatches.

Verified (tests/test_clos.c test_print_object_kernel): a user method dispatches on
a READTABLE; a stream with no method still prints #<...STREAM id>; (find-package
"KEYWORD") prints #<PACKAGE "KEYWORD"> and signals PRINT-NOT-READABLE under
*PRINT-READABLY*; ordinary objects (conses, numbers, strings, symbols) unaffected.
Full suite green.  Issue complete.
